</div>
<div>
<div class="faq-list"><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>What plate size is best for most people?</summary><p class="faq-answer">A 1-inch plate is the safest default. It is versatile enough for most hair lengths, touch-ups, and some curling or waving, while still being manageable around roots and face-framing pieces.</p></details><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>Is higher heat better for straightening hair?</summary><p class="faq-answer">No. Higher heat can increase damage risk. Controlled, consistent heat and smooth plate glide usually matter more than the highest temperature number.</p></details><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>Is ceramic or titanium better?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Ceramic tends to feel gentler and more forgiving, while titanium can feel faster and more powerful. The better choice depends on hair type, but plate quality and heat control matter more than the material label alone.</p></details><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>Can a flat iron damage hair if used regularly?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Yes. Frequent heat styling can cause damage, especially with high heat, repeated passes, or poor technique. Heat protectant, lower settings, and smoother plates help reduce risk.</p></details><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>Is a mini straightener enough as a main straightener?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Usually no. Mini straighteners are useful for travel, bangs, roots, and short sections, but they are slow and limiting for full-head regular styling.</p></details><details class="faq-item"><summary>How much should most people spend on a hair straightener?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Most people should avoid both the cheapest tools and unnecessary luxury pricing. A strong midrange or professional-style flat iron is usually the best balance of performance, control, and ownership confidence.</p></details><details class="faq-item"><summary>Is titanium good for thick or coarse hair?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Titanium can be a strong fit for thicker, coarser, or harder-to-style hair because it tends to feel faster and more powerful. Buyers with fine or fragile hair should use lower heat and avoid repeated passes.</p></details><details class="faq-item"><summary>How do I reduce frizz when straightening?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Start with dry hair, use reasonable section sizes, apply heat protectant, avoid excessive heat, and choose a tool with smooth plates and stable temperature control.</p></details>
